29
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,860
Now he's very much involved with philanthropy, and doing

30
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:37,860
things like lecturing young people, which he freely does

31
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:40,640
out of a sense of commitment.

32
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:41,970
So, I think he's a

33
00:01:41,970 --> 00:01:43,300
committed person that has a moral

34
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:43,780
purpose.

35
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:46,520
That's why I asked him

36
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:49,260
to be one of our lecturers.

37
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:51,610
I took an offer from

38
00:01:51,610 --> 00:01:53,960
the national bank, which is a

39
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:55,460
little bit too expensive.

40
00:01:55,460 --> 00:01:58,760
But today, we're talking about banks, and I wanted to

41
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:02,500
outline of my lecture, I'm going to start with the

42
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:06,400
origins of banks, thousands of years ago.

43
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:12,340
Then I'll talk about the theory of banks, fractional

44
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:15,500
reserves and deposit insurance.

45
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:17,260
Then bank regulation.

46
00:02:17,260 --> 00:02:18,180
Particularly, in light of the

47
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:19,100
world financial crisis, which is

48
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:19,220
an economic crisis.

49
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:23,180
which has changed the nature of regulation, changes that

50
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:26,460
will be with us for decades to come.

51
00:02:26,460 --> 00:02:30,500
I'll talk about shadow banking, which is the new

52
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:35,280
banking sector that emerged and escaped regulation until

53
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:36,740
the crisis.

54
00:02:36,740 --> 00:02:38,560
And then I'll talk at the

55
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:40,380
end a little bit about comparison

56
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:46,180
of financial crises in the past in various places around

57
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:52,340
the world to see how banks managed in those crises.

58
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:55,330
So, first thing I want to

59
00:02:55,330 --> 00:02:58,320
say, this is a lecture about banks.

60
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:02,840
And that means traditional banks who take

61
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:05,640
deposits and lend money.

62
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:07,640
It's not about investment banks.

63
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:09,080
That's another lecture.

64
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:13,620
An investment bank, a pure investment bank, does not

65
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:15,460
accept deposits.

66
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:16,060
And it's most

67
00:03:16,180 --> 00:03:20,940
The most characteristic thing is underwriting of securities.

68
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:23,040
So, that's a different lecture.

69
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:26,740
And I'm not talking about central banks in this lecture.

70
00:03:26,740 --> 00:03:29,940
That's also a lecture on monetary policy.

71
00:03:29,940 --> 00:03:34,440
Central banks are the government organizations that

72
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:38,520
manage the money supply of a country.

73
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:43,820
OK.

74
00:03:43,820 --> 00:03:46,060
So, we're talking here about banks.

75
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:48,440
And I thought I should

76
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:50,820
start out by defining a bank.

77
00:03:50,820 --> 00:03:59,400
The word bank, by the way, means counter or tabletop,

78
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:01,820
where bankers used to do their business.

79
00:04:01,820 --> 00:04:06,820
That's the English word that emerged in the 15th century.

80
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:12,820
But banks, of course, precede that with other names.

81
00:04:12,820 --> 00:04:15,940
What is it that is the characteristic of a bank?

82
00:04:15,940 --> 00:04:16,940
It's the activity of banks.

83
00:04:16,940 --> 00:04:22,940
I would say, maybe the most characteristic thing is that

84
00:04:22,940 --> 00:04:24,940
banks earn spread income.

85
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:28,440
That is, they borrow at a

86
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:31,940
lower interest rate and lend it out

87
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:33,940
at a higher interest rate, and they make the difference.

88
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:36,940
Your deposit rate is lower than

89
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:39,940
the rate at which they charge

90
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:40,940
for the loans they make.

91
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:44,940
So, that's the spread income of a bank.

92
00:04:44,940 --> 00:04:45,820
OK.

93
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:47,320
And then, the other aspect of

94
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:48,820
banks is that they borrow at a

95
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:49,820
lower rate than they lend.

96
00:04:49,820 --> 00:04:51,320
So, that's the idea of a bank,

97
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:52,820
that you borrow at a lower rate

98
00:04:52,820 --> 00:04:53,820
than you lend.

99
00:04:53,820 --> 00:04:56,320
So, that might be considered the

100
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:58,820
core idea of a bank, that

101
00:04:58,820 --> 00:05:01,820
you borrow at a lower rate than you lend.

102
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:03,820
But I'm not sure that that summarizes it either.

103
00:05:03,820 --> 00:05:08,820
There's other aspects of banks that we'll talk about.

104
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:14,820
Another aspect of banks, traditionally, has been note

105
00:05:14,820 --> 00:05:17,820
that they print money.

106
00:05:17,820 --> 00:05:22,820
That is, they print paper money, and then it circulates

107
00:05:22,820 --> 00:05:27,820
and goes, you have some of these in your pocket.

108
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:30,820
They're currency.

109
00:05:30,820 --> 00:05:32,320
If you stopped a person on

110
00:05:32,320 --> 00:05:33,820
the street a couple hundred years

111
00:05:33,820 --> 00:05:37,820
ago and said, what is the essence of a bank?

112
00:05:37,820 --> 00:05:39,320
I suspect the first thing they

113
00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:40,820
would say is, oh, they print

114
00:05:40,820 --> 00:05:43,820
money, and that's the paper money that we use.

115
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:45,820
You don't think of it this way.

116
00:05:45,820 --> 00:05:50,820
Probably not, because most of that function all over the

117
00:05:50,820 --> 00:05:54,820
world has been shifted to the government banks, the central

118
00:05:54,820 --> 00:05:58,820
banks in the various countries.

119
00:05:58,820 --> 00:06:01,320
And so, you don't think

120
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:03,820
of private banks as issuing bank

121
00:06:03,820 --> 00:06:05,820
notes.

122
00:06:05,820 --> 00:06:08,820
But they used to, and it used to be prominent.

123
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:11,320
The private bank issuing of notes

124
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:13,820
today in the world is a

125
00:06:13,820 --> 00:06:18,820
bank that I believe is concentrated primarily in two

126
00:06:18,820 --> 00:06:20,820
countries.

127
00:06:20,820 --> 00:06:22,820
One of them is the United

128
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:24,820
Kingdom, and the other one is

129
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:26,820
Hong Kong.

130
00:06:26,820 --> 00:06:28,820
Tell me if there's another country.

131
00:06:28,820 --> 00:06:30,820
I don't know who else does it.

132
00:06:30,820 --> 00:06:33,820
In the United Kingdom, there are eight banks that still

133
00:06:33,820 --> 00:06:35,820
print pound sterling notes.

134
00:06:35,820 --> 00:06:40,820
And they're not very prominent.

135
00:06:40,820 --> 00:06:41,820
In Scotland.

136
00:06:42,820 --> 00:06:45,820
There are some.

137
00:06:45,820 --> 00:06:49,820
I actually once went through the Channel from Scotland to

138
00:06:49,820 --> 00:06:52,320
France, and I tried to spend

139
00:06:52,320 --> 00:06:54,820
my Scottish sterling notes on the

140
00:06:54,820 --> 00:06:56,820
train, and the guy who

141
00:06:56,820 --> 00:06:58,820
was French speaking stared at it

142
00:06:58,820 --> 00:07:00,820
and said, what's this?

143
00:07:00,820 --> 00:07:02,820
And he wouldn't take it.

144
00:07:02,820 --> 00:07:04,820
So, I said, this is pound sterling.

145
00:07:04,820 --> 00:07:05,820
This is official.

146
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:10,820
This trades at par with the Bank of England notes.

147
00:07:11,820 --> 00:07:20,820
Hong Kong has three banks that issue their bank notes.

148
00:07:20,820 --> 00:07:24,820
The Hong Kong and Shanghai Bank Corporation, the Standard

149
00:07:24,820 --> 00:07:29,820
Charter Bank, and the Bank of China Hong Kong.

150
00:07:29,820 --> 00:07:31,820
Everywhere else, banks don't issue notes.

151
00:07:31,820 --> 00:07:33,820
And the reason they don't is

152
00:07:33,820 --> 00:07:35,820
laws have been made to prevent

153
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:39,820
them, because these notes lost value so many times in

154
00:07:39,820 --> 00:07:40,820
financial crises.

155
00:07:40,820 --> 00:07:43,820
And the government said, it's not something that will allow

156
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:45,820
private banks to do.

157
00:07:45,820 --> 00:07:48,820
So, it's generally gone.

158
00:07:48,820 --> 00:07:50,820
If you look at your $1 bills

159
00:07:50,820 --> 00:07:52,820
in your pocket, it will give the

160
00:07:52,820 --> 00:07:55,820
name of a Federal Reserve Bank.

161
00:07:55,820 --> 00:07:57,820
And there's 12 of them.

162
00:07:57,820 --> 00:07:59,320
Our Federal Reserve Bank here

163
00:07:59,320 --> 00:08:00,820
is the Federal Reserve Bank of

164
00:08:00,820 --> 00:08:01,820
Boston.

165
00:08:01,820 --> 00:08:03,320
So, you can look at your

166
00:08:03,320 --> 00:08:04,820
dollar bills and see which bank issued

167
00:08:04,820 --> 00:08:05,820
it.

168
00:08:05,820 --> 00:08:08,820
So, you're probably carrying a lot of Boston money.

169
00:08:08,820 --> 00:08:09,820
But. . .

170
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:12,820
Since it's all the government, you don't even notice that,

171
00:08:12,820 --> 00:08:13,820
right?

172
00:08:13,820 --> 00:08:14,820
You don't notice.

173
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:16,320
If this were 1750 and you

174
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:17,820
had Boston money in your pocket,

175
00:08:17,820 --> 00:08:19,820
you might, because we're in Connecticut, you might have a

176
00:08:19,820 --> 00:08:20,820
problem.

177
00:08:20,820 --> 00:08:21,820
You'd have to go to a

178
00:08:21,820 --> 00:08:22,820
money dealer and have it exchanged,

179
00:08:22,820 --> 00:08:26,820
because they didn't know or trust the Boston bank.

180
00:08:26,820 --> 00:08:27,820
That's all past.

181
00:08:27,820 --> 00:08:31,820
So, note issue is a matter of history.

182
00:08:31,820 --> 00:08:34,819
So, there's other aspects of

183
00:08:34,819 --> 00:08:37,819
a bank I want to emphasize.

184
00:08:37,819 --> 00:08:38,819
One is. . .

185
00:08:39,820 --> 00:08:40,820
liquidity.

186
00:08:40,820 --> 00:08:48,820
And this is an essential element of banking as well.

187
00:08:48,820 --> 00:08:50,320
And I'll come back to that

188
00:08:50,320 --> 00:08:51,820
when we talk about theory in a

189
00:08:51,820 --> 00:08:52,820
minute.

190
00:08:52,820 --> 00:08:56,820
But banks offer liquidity by

191
00:08:56,820 --> 00:09:00,820
borrowing short and lending long.

192
00:09:00,820 --> 00:09:03,820
This is different from spread income.

193
00:09:03,820 --> 00:09:05,320
I'm saying the interest rate is

194
00:09:05,320 --> 00:09:06,820
lower on the borrowing of the

195
00:09:06,820 --> 00:09:07,820
bank than the lending of the bank.

196
00:09:07,820 --> 00:09:08,320
The interest rate is lower on the borrowing

197
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:08,820
of the bank than the lending of the bank.

198
00:09:08,820 --> 00:09:09,820
There's another discrepancy.

199
00:09:09,820 --> 00:09:13,320
The maturity is longer on the lending of

200
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:16,820
the bank than the borrowing of the bank.

201
00:09:16,820 --> 00:09:18,820
So, banks are providers of liquidity.

202
00:09:18,820 --> 00:09:21,820
That means a business wants to borrow money.

203
00:09:21,820 --> 00:09:23,320
Or let's say a homeowner wants

204
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:24,820
to borrow money to buy a home.

205
00:09:24,820 --> 00:09:25,820
Right?

206
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:26,820
Let's take that example.

207
00:09:26,820 --> 00:09:27,820
That's more familiar.

208
00:09:27,820 --> 00:09:29,320
You're going to lock up

209
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:30,820
the money for maybe 30 years.

210
00:09:30,820 --> 00:09:33,820
You don't want to pay the loan back tomorrow.

211
00:09:33,820 --> 00:09:34,820
What if the lender says, I

212
00:09:34,820 --> 00:09:35,820
need the money, give it back?

213
00:09:35,820 --> 00:09:37,820
You can't give it back.

214
00:09:37,820 --> 00:09:38,820
You don't want to give it back.

215
00:09:38,820 --> 00:09:43,320
So, what a bank does is it takes

216
00:09:43,320 --> 00:09:47,820
deposits and allows people to cash them in whenever

217
00:09:47,820 --> 00:09:49,820
they want.

218
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:51,820
It lends the money out

219
00:09:51,820 --> 00:09:53,820
long on 30-year or so loans.

220
00:09:53,820 --> 00:09:55,820
So, it generates liquidity.

221
00:09:55,820 --> 00:09:58,320
The borrower has what he or

222
00:09:58,320 --> 00:10:00,820
she wants, which is a 30-year loan.

223
00:10:00,820 --> 00:10:03,820
The lender has what they want.

224
00:10:03,820 --> 00:10:05,820
They have a loan.

225
00:10:05,820 --> 00:10:06,820
They have a loan.

226
00:10:06,820 --> 00:10:10,820
They have an account they can get at any time.

227
00:10:10,820 --> 00:10:13,820
But the problem with it – so, this is

228
00:10:13,820 --> 00:10:16,820
an important function of banks – but the problem

229
00:10:16,820 --> 00:10:22,820
with it is that there's a problem of crises.

230
00:10:22,820 --> 00:10:25,820
Because, if everybody asks to pull their

231
00:10:25,820 --> 00:10:28,820
money out at once, they can't do it.

232
00:10:28,820 --> 00:10:31,820
The banks, in normal circumstances, generate

233
00:10:31,820 --> 00:10:34,820
liquidity, but they create a system that's

234
00:10:34,820 --> 00:10:35,820
vulnerable.

235
00:10:35,820 --> 00:10:42,440
And so, the banking industry has been plagued by frequent

236
00:10:42,440 --> 00:10:45,580
crises throughout history.

237
00:10:45,580 --> 00:10:48,740
So, I wanted to say something about the very

238
00:10:48,740 --> 00:10:50,240
beginnings of banking.

239
00:10:50,240 --> 00:10:51,880
Who did it first?

240
00:10:51,880 --> 00:10:55,660
Well, by the way, I should have written the word

241
00:10:55,660 --> 00:10:57,460
interest rate.

242
00:10:57,460 --> 00:11:00,140
When I'm talking about spread income or margin, I'm talking

243
00:11:00,140 --> 00:11:02,980
about the difference between two interest rates.

244
00:11:02,980 --> 00:11:09,220
But more primitive is the idea of interest rate.

245
00:11:09,220 --> 00:11:12,260
So, where did that idea come from?

246
00:11:12,260 --> 00:11:17,600
Well, I was reading some economic historians.

247
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:22,540
Apparently, the word interest first appeared in the Sumerian

248
00:11:22,540 --> 00:11:23,780
language.

249
00:11:26,780 --> 00:11:31,000
I guess the oldest records are about 2000 BC.

250
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:31,980
And they had a word, which

251
00:11:31,980 --> 00:11:32,960
I don't know if you've ever

252
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:33,960
heard of.

253
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:38,900
They write as M-A-S, I guess that's pronounced mosh, which

254
00:11:38,900 --> 00:11:40,000
was their word for interest.

255
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,060
But it also means lamb.

256
00:11:42,060 --> 00:11:45,100
And so, one historian was wondering, why do the

257
00:11:45,100 --> 00:11:47,810
Sumerians use the same word for

258
00:11:47,810 --> 00:11:50,520
interest as they do for an

259
00:11:50,520 --> 00:11:51,980
animal, a lamb?

260
00:11:51,980 --> 00:11:54,680
And he thought that maybe it

261
00:11:54,680 --> 00:11:57,380
was because the idea of lending

262
00:11:57,380 --> 00:11:59,790
money at interest grew out of

263
00:11:59,790 --> 00:12:02,200
an earlier idea of renting land.

264
00:12:02,960 --> 00:12:04,660
So, you would have land

265
00:12:04,660 --> 00:12:06,360
that you weren't using in ancient

266
00:12:06,360 --> 00:12:08,940
Sumeria, and you would rent it

267
00:12:08,940 --> 00:12:11,520
out to somebody who would then

268
00:12:11,520 --> 00:12:12,220
use it.

269
00:12:12,220 --> 00:12:13,400
One of the things that the

270
00:12:13,400 --> 00:12:14,580
person would do with it is

271
00:12:14,580 --> 00:12:18,080
graze livestock, graze sheep on the land.

272
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:19,860
So, you would say, well, I

273
00:12:19,860 --> 00:12:21,640
need some produce from the land to

274
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:24,520
compensate me for letting you use it.

275
00:12:24,520 --> 00:12:25,440
So, the guy would say,

276
00:12:25,440 --> 00:12:26,360
fine, I'll give you all the

