And so, we had a

820
00:35:35,960 --> 00:35:37,960
huge crisis in the United States,

821
00:35:37,960 --> 00:35:43,960
called the saving and loan crisis in the 1980s.

822
00:35:43,960 --> 00:35:48,960
So, the S&L crisis

823
00:35:48,960 --> 00:35:52,960
in the 1980s

824
00:35:52,960 --> 00:35:55,960
was

825
00:35:55,960 --> 00:35:59,960
due to a widespread failure of saving and loan associations.

826
00:35:59,960 --> 00:36:01,460
And then, kind of a

827
00:36:01,460 --> 00:36:02,960
run on the saving and loan.

828
00:36:02,960 --> 00:36:05,460
But, it wasn't really a

829
00:36:05,460 --> 00:36:07,960
run, because the FSLIC was trusted.

830
00:36:07,960 --> 00:36:13,960
And what ended up happening is, the FSLIC had reserves

831
00:36:13,960 --> 00:36:16,960
against a certain amount of losses from the banks.

832
00:36:16,960 --> 00:36:18,960
But they went through them completely.

833
00:36:18,960 --> 00:36:20,960
And then, they were bankrupt.

834
00:36:20,960 --> 00:36:22,960
So, the insurer went bankrupt.

835
00:36:22,960 --> 00:36:24,960
What then happened is, the United

836
00:36:24,960 --> 00:36:26,960
States government picked up the tab.

837
00:36:26,960 --> 00:36:28,960
And the total tab was

838
00:36:28,960 --> 00:36:31,960
$150 billion.

839
00:36:31,960 --> 00:36:33,960
So,

840
00:36:33,960 --> 00:36:35,960
and that restored confidence.

841
00:36:35,960 --> 00:36:37,960
I guess the government had to do that.

842
00:36:37,960 --> 00:36:39,960
So, what you really,

843
00:36:39,960 --> 00:36:41,960
and the FSLIC no longer exists.

844
00:36:41,960 --> 00:36:44,460
Savings and loans are now insured

845
00:36:44,460 --> 00:36:46,960
by the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation.

846
00:36:46,960 --> 00:36:49,960
So, what, you know, these institutions

847
00:36:49,960 --> 00:36:52,960
really don't necessarily represent the real insurance.

848
00:36:52,960 --> 00:36:55,960
You have to always see beyond institutions.

849
00:36:55,960 --> 00:36:57,960
The FSLIC was

850
00:36:57,960 --> 00:36:59,960
encouraging people to believe in

851
00:36:59,960 --> 00:37:01,960
the security of the banking system.

852
00:37:01,960 --> 00:37:03,960
But it really wasn't the ultimate security.

853
00:37:03,960 --> 00:37:06,960
The ultimate security wasn't even written down.

854
00:37:06,960 --> 00:37:11,960
It was the recognition by the U.S. government

855
00:37:11,960 --> 00:37:14,960
that if we let FSLIC fail,

856
00:37:14,960 --> 00:37:16,960
and we let all these depositors in

857
00:37:16,960 --> 00:37:18,960
the savings and loans lose their money,

858
00:37:18,960 --> 00:37:20,960
it's going to destroy the confidence that

859
00:37:20,960 --> 00:37:22,960
has kept us away from bank runs.

860
00:37:22,960 --> 00:37:24,960
And the next thing, the commercial banks,

861
00:37:24,960 --> 00:37:26,960
or who knows, what else will happen.

862
00:37:27,960 --> 00:37:30,960
So, what always happens is

863
00:37:30,960 --> 00:37:32,960
the government stands behind these promises,

864
00:37:32,960 --> 00:37:35,960
even if they weren't made so clearly.

865
00:37:37,960 --> 00:37:39,960
There's another example

866
00:37:39,960 --> 00:37:42,960
I'll give you, which is more recent

867
00:37:42,960 --> 00:37:44,960
of a bank run.

868
00:37:44,960 --> 00:37:47,960
And that occurred in the United Kingdom

869
00:37:47,960 --> 00:37:50,960
in 2007.

870
00:37:50,960 --> 00:37:52,960
The bank was called Northern Rock.

871
00:37:57,960 --> 00:38:01,960
And a rumor started,

872
00:38:01,960 --> 00:38:03,960
this was at the beginning of the financial crisis,

873
00:38:03,960 --> 00:38:05,960
the rumor started that Northern Rock

874
00:38:05,960 --> 00:38:07,960
held a lot of subprime securities

875
00:38:07,960 --> 00:38:09,960
and was going to go bankrupt.

876
00:38:09,960 --> 00:38:11,960
So, people rushed to Northern Rock

877
00:38:11,960 --> 00:38:13,960
and big lines formed outside of Northern Rock.

878
00:38:13,960 --> 00:38:16,960
They wanted to get there first,

879
00:38:16,960 --> 00:38:17,960
because you thought, well, they're

880
00:38:17,960 --> 00:38:18,960
still handing out the money.

881
00:38:18,960 --> 00:38:20,960
I want to be there first.

882
00:38:20,960 --> 00:38:22,960
And then newspaper photographers photographed

883
00:38:22,960 --> 00:38:24,960
the crowd outside the bank.

884
00:38:24,960 --> 00:38:26,960
And people thought, well, this is just like 1933,

885
00:38:26,960 --> 00:38:29,960
in the huge banking crisis.

886
00:38:29,960 --> 00:38:31,960
And so,

887
00:38:31,960 --> 00:38:34,960
now actually,

888
00:38:34,960 --> 00:38:41,960
the UK government did have deposit insurance.

889
00:38:41,960 --> 00:38:45,960
But the deposit insurance in the UK

890
00:38:45,960 --> 00:38:51,960
would insure fully all deposits up to 3,000 pounds.

891
00:38:51,960 --> 00:38:56,960
And then, it gave 90%

892
00:38:56,960 --> 00:39:05,960
of the deposit insurance up to 75,000 pounds.

893
00:39:05,960 --> 00:39:07,960
After that, you were out of luck.

894
00:39:07,960 --> 00:39:08,960
Okay?

895
00:39:08,960 --> 00:39:10,960
So, why was there a bank run on Northern Rock

896
00:39:10,960 --> 00:39:12,960
when there was deposit insurance?

897
00:39:12,960 --> 00:39:13,960
Well, I'll tell you.

898
00:39:13,960 --> 00:39:15,960
It was people who had more

899
00:39:15,960 --> 00:39:17,960
than 3,000 pounds in the bank.

900
00:39:17,960 --> 00:39:19,960
Very simple.

901
00:39:19,960 --> 00:39:20,960
And so,

902
00:39:20,960 --> 00:39:23,960
you know, they didn't really have enough deposit insurance

903
00:39:23,960 --> 00:39:24,960
to stop a run.

904
00:39:24,960 --> 00:39:26,960
So, Mervyn King,

905
00:39:26,960 --> 00:39:28,960
the head of the Bank of England,

906
00:39:28,960 --> 00:39:29,960
just decided,

907
00:39:29,960 --> 00:39:30,960
you know what?

908
00:39:30,960 --> 00:39:32,960
We'll bail everybody out.

909
00:39:32,960 --> 00:39:33,960
No questions.

910
00:39:33,960 --> 00:39:35,960
Forget our deposit insurance scheme.

911
00:39:35,960 --> 00:39:37,960
And that stopped the run.

912
00:39:37,960 --> 00:39:38,960
So, again,

913
00:39:38,960 --> 00:39:41,960
it happened the same way in the United Kingdom.

914
00:39:41,960 --> 00:39:43,960
The deposit insurance

915
00:39:43,960 --> 00:39:45,960
stopped it before

916
00:39:45,960 --> 00:39:47,960
there was any problem.

917
00:39:47,960 --> 00:39:49,960
So, the United Kingdom

918
00:39:49,960 --> 00:39:52,960
has never had a bank run failure

919
00:39:52,960 --> 00:39:54,960
since 1866.

920
00:39:54,960 --> 00:39:56,960
And that's because,

921
00:39:56,960 --> 00:39:59,960
it's not really because of any deposit insurance scheme.

922
00:39:59,960 --> 00:40:01,960
It's about the Bank of England,

923
00:40:01,960 --> 00:40:03,960
which is their central bank,

924
00:40:03,960 --> 00:40:06,960
and what it does to maintain confidence.

925
00:40:06,960 --> 00:40:08,960
Other countries handle it differently.

926
00:40:08,960 --> 00:40:10,960
I was going to mention,

927
00:40:10,960 --> 00:40:11,960
in Germany,

928
00:40:11,960 --> 00:40:14,960
IKB,

929
00:40:14,960 --> 00:40:20,960
Deutsche Industriebank.

930
00:40:20,960 --> 00:40:25,960
Okay.

931
00:40:25,960 --> 00:40:30,960
In 2007,

932
00:40:30,960 --> 00:40:32,960
this is a German bank

933
00:40:32,960 --> 00:40:34,960
that German depositors put their money in.

934
00:40:34,960 --> 00:40:39,960
And it had invested a lot in subprime securities.

935
00:40:39,960 --> 00:40:40,960
And it was in trouble.

936
00:40:40,960 --> 00:40:42,460
And there was starting to be

937
00:40:42,460 --> 00:40:43,960
worries of a run on them.

938
00:40:43,960 --> 00:40:46,960
The German government didn't even wait

939
00:40:46,960 --> 00:40:47,960
for a bank run.

940
00:40:47,960 --> 00:40:49,960
They just bailed them out.

941
00:40:49,960 --> 00:40:51,960
And that cost them

942
00:40:51,960 --> 00:40:57,960
1.5 billion euros.

943
00:40:57,960 --> 00:40:58,960
But again,

944
00:40:58,960 --> 00:41:00,960
the governments know

945
00:41:00,960 --> 00:41:02,960
that they want to maintain confidence.

946
00:41:02,960 --> 00:41:03,960
And so, they do it.

947
00:41:03,960 --> 00:41:06,960
They do what they have to do.

948
00:41:06,960 --> 00:41:08,960
Now, I want to go to

949
00:41:08,960 --> 00:41:09,960
bank regulation

950
00:41:09,960 --> 00:41:10,960
more generally.

951
00:41:10,960 --> 00:41:13,960
If you are insuring banks,

952
00:41:13,960 --> 00:41:16,960
then you better regulate them.

953
00:41:16,960 --> 00:41:18,960
Because there's a moral hazard problem.

954
00:41:18,960 --> 00:41:20,960
I described a moral hazard problem

955
00:41:20,960 --> 00:41:23,960
for a company that borrows money from a bank.

956
00:41:23,960 --> 00:41:25,960
But there's moral hazard problems

957
00:41:25,960 --> 00:41:27,960
for banks as well.

958
00:41:27,960 --> 00:41:28,960
Namely,

959
00:41:28,960 --> 00:41:29,960
banks can do the same trick.

960
00:41:29,960 --> 00:41:30,960
I said,

961
00:41:30,960 --> 00:41:31,960
go to the racetrack.

962
00:41:31,960 --> 00:41:33,960
Borrow money and go to the racetrack.

963
00:41:33,960 --> 00:41:35,960
Banks can do that.

964
00:41:35,960 --> 00:41:36,960
Same thing.

965
00:41:36,960 --> 00:41:37,960
I said,

966
00:41:37,960 --> 00:41:38,960
they wouldn't actually go to the racetrack.

967
00:41:38,960 --> 00:41:40,960
They would pick some really risky business venture.

968
00:41:40,960 --> 00:41:42,960
And if it fails,

969
00:41:42,960 --> 00:41:45,960
then it all falls to the deposit insurer.

970
00:41:45,960 --> 00:41:48,960
This is a fundamental lesson of insurance.

971
00:41:48,960 --> 00:41:50,960
Whenever you insure something,

972
00:41:50,960 --> 00:41:53,960
you've got to regulate the person insured.

973
00:41:53,960 --> 00:41:56,960
Because once you've taken a risk from their shoulders,

974
00:41:56,960 --> 00:41:59,960
you create moral hazard for them.

975
00:41:59,960 --> 00:42:00,960
And so,

976
00:42:00,960 --> 00:42:04,960
bank regulation is very important.

977
00:42:04,960 --> 00:42:07,960
So, I was going to talk mostly here about

978
00:42:07,960 --> 00:42:10,460
the kind of bank regulation

979
00:42:10,460 --> 00:42:12,960
that has an international dimension.

980
00:42:12,960 --> 00:42:13,960
And so,

981
00:42:13,960 --> 00:42:16,960
what I wanted to talk about is the Basel

982
00:42:16,960 --> 00:42:18,960
bank regulations

983
00:42:18,960 --> 00:42:25,960
that were generated by an international organization

984
00:42:25,960 --> 00:42:33,960
in Basel, Switzerland.

985
00:42:33,960 --> 00:42:36,460
After the saving of loan

986
00:42:36,460 --> 00:42:38,960
crisis was the first Basel I

987
00:42:38,960 --> 00:42:42,960
was an international meeting

988
00:42:42,960 --> 00:42:46,960
that published a set of recommendations

989
00:42:46,960 --> 00:42:48,960
for all the countries of the world

990
00:42:48,960 --> 00:42:50,960
to regulate their banks.

991
00:42:50,960 --> 00:42:51,960
The idea was that

992
00:42:51,960 --> 00:42:54,960
there should be some coherence across countries.

993
00:42:54,960 --> 00:42:57,960
If one country regulates its bank very stiffly,

994
00:42:57,960 --> 00:43:00,960
that's going to drive business out of that country

995
00:43:00,960 --> 00:43:01,960
into other countries.

996
00:43:01,960 --> 00:43:02,960
Also,

997
00:43:02,960 --> 00:43:04,960
there should be some standardization.

998
00:43:04,960 --> 00:43:05,960
It helps the world economy

999
00:43:05,960 --> 00:43:08,960
if everybody knows that all the banks of the world

1000
00:43:08,960 --> 00:43:10,960
have similar regulations.

1001
00:43:10,960 --> 00:43:11,960
But Basel,

1002
00:43:11,960 --> 00:43:12,960
the Basel committee,

1003
00:43:12,960 --> 00:43:13,960
as it's called,

1004
00:43:13,960 --> 00:43:15,960
that created the recommendations

1005
00:43:15,960 --> 00:43:17,960
had no legal authority.

1006
00:43:17,960 --> 00:43:20,960
All it could do is recommend.

1007
00:43:20,960 --> 00:43:23,960
But it did recommend bank regulations

1008
00:43:23,960 --> 00:43:27,960
and these were widely adopted around the world.

1009
00:43:27,960 --> 00:43:32,960
Each country could make modifications,

1010
00:43:32,960 --> 00:43:33,960
whatever they want.

1011
00:43:33,960 --> 00:43:35,960
You can't order countries around.

1012
00:43:35,960 --> 00:43:39,960
But they often followed the Basel recommendation.

1013
00:43:39,960 --> 00:43:41,960
In Basel II,

1014
00:43:41,960 --> 00:43:45,960
they met again in the 2000s

1015
00:43:45,960 --> 00:43:47,960
and they issued recommendations.

1016
00:43:47,960 --> 00:43:49,960
In 2004,

1017
00:43:49,960 --> 00:43:52,960
what they said in Basel II

1018
00:43:52,960 --> 00:43:54,960
was the banking system was getting

1019
00:43:54,960 --> 00:43:56,960
so much more complicated

1020
00:43:56,960 --> 00:43:57,960
that they had to think

1021
00:43:57,960 --> 00:43:58,960
more about how to do it.

1022
00:43:58,960 --> 00:44:00,960
Now there's all these complicated derivatives

1023
00:44:00,960 --> 00:44:03,960
and special purpose vehicles.

1024
00:44:03,960 --> 00:44:08,960
And so they had to update their regulation.

1025
00:44:08,960 --> 00:44:09,960
Unfortunately,

1026
00:44:09,960 --> 00:44:12,960
Basel II has suffered a reputation blow

1027
00:44:12,960 --> 00:44:14,960
because right after Basel II,

1028
00:44:14,960 --> 00:44:16,960
we had the world financial crisis.

1029
00:44:16,960 --> 00:44:18,960
So they didn't do something right.

1030
00:44:18,960 --> 00:44:21,960
They didn't really fix the system in Basel II.

1031
00:44:21,960 --> 00:44:24,960
So that brought us to Basel III,

1032
00:44:24,960 --> 00:44:29,960
which is the latest version.

1033
00:44:29,960 --> 00:44:33,960
And they issued their report in 2010.

1034
00:44:33,960 --> 00:44:35,960
And also in 2010,

1035
00:44:35,960 --> 00:44:40,960
the G20 nations meeting in Seoul, Korea,

1036
00:44:40,960 --> 00:44:45,960
expressed their support for Basel III.

1037
00:44:45,960 --> 00:44:51,960
So Basel III is the current world regulation standard.

1038
00:44:51,960 --> 00:44:57,960
But it's phased in gradually.

1039
00:44:57,960 --> 00:45:01,960
And it won't be fully phased in until 2019.

1040
00:45:01,960 --> 00:45:04,960
They didn't want to put it in all at once

1041
00:45:04,960 --> 00:45:06,960
because the world is in a financial crisis

1042
00:45:06,960 --> 00:45:08,960
and it would be too stressful.

1043
00:45:08,960 --> 00:45:11,960
So it has a slow phase in.

1044
00:45:11,960 --> 00:45:13,960
And

1045
00:45:15,960 --> 00:45:18,960
some of the details haven't even been worked out yet.

1046
00:45:18,960 --> 00:45:19,960
They've recommended,

1047
00:45:19,960 --> 00:45:22,960
the G20 countries have agreed in principle

1048
00:45:22,960 --> 00:45:25,960
that these regulations are where we'll go.

1049
00:45:25,960 --> 00:45:28,960
But details have yet to be worked out.

1050
00:45:28,960 --> 00:45:31,960
But I wanted to,

1051
00:45:31,960 --> 00:45:33,960
you know, bank regulation is a big business.

1052
00:45:33,960 --> 00:45:34,960
We could spend,

1053
00:45:34,960 --> 00:45:36,960
we could have a whole semester

1054
00:45:36,960 --> 00:45:38,960
studying what these guys do.

1055
00:45:38,960 --> 00:45:40,460
So I just wanted to give

1056
00:45:40,460 --> 00:45:41,960
you a kind of a caricature

1057
00:45:41,960 --> 00:45:43,960
of what's in Basel.

1058
00:45:43,960 --> 00:45:44,960
It's also in Fibozzi.

1059
00:45:44,960 --> 00:45:46,960
The Fibozzi textbook,

1060
00:45:46,960 --> 00:45:48,960
which is copyright 2010,

1061
00:45:48,960 --> 00:45:50,960
you'd think this would be in it,

1062
00:45:50,960 --> 00:45:51,960
but it's not.

1063
00:45:51,960 --> 00:45:54,960
Basel I and Basel II are in Fibozzi.

1064
00:45:54,960 --> 00:45:56,960
Basel III isn't because it didn't come in

1065
00:45:56,960 --> 00:45:58,960
until the end of 2010.

1066
00:45:58,960 --> 00:46:00,960
And so the book was,

1067
00:46:00,960 --> 00:46:02,960
it didn't make it in time

1068
00:46:02,960 --> 00:46:05,960
to appear in your book.

1069
00:46:05,960 --> 00:46:07,960
But I wanted to just give you a,

1070
00:46:07,960 --> 00:46:12,960
just a simple account of

1071
00:46:12,960 --> 00:46:14,960
all the Basel agreements.

1072
00:46:14,960 --> 00:46:18,960
And some sense of where we are with Basel III.

1073
00:46:18,960 --> 00:46:23,960
They're all about banks having enough money.

1074
00:46:23,960 --> 00:46:25,960
You know, not taking on too much,

1075
00:46:25,960 --> 00:46:28,960
enough money for the risks they take.

1076
00:46:28,960 --> 00:46:30,960
Let me start with Basel I.

1077
00:46:30,960 --> 00:46:36,960
And because part of Basel I

1078
00:46:36,960 --> 00:46:39,960
is enforced in all three of them.

1079
00:46:39,960 --> 00:46:43,960
And there's a concept called risk weighted risk.

1080
00:46:44,960 --> 00:46:48,960
And that's the risk weighted asset,

1081
00:46:48,960 --> 00:46:51,960
which is in Basel I.

1082
00:46:51,960 --> 00:46:54,960
And Basel II and Basel III,

1083
00:46:54,960 --> 00:46:57,960
essentially the same.

1084
00:46:57,960 --> 00:47:01,960
Okay, so here's the idea.

1085
00:47:01,960 --> 00:47:04,960
We're going to put capital requirements

1086
00:47:04,960 --> 00:47:07,960
based on risk weighted asset.

1087
00:47:07,960 --> 00:47:08,960
And what does that mean?

1088
00:47:08,960 --> 00:47:13,960
That means that banks cannot take too many risks.

1089
00:47:13,960 --> 00:47:15,960
They have to have enough money

1090
00:47:15,960 --> 00:47:17,960
to back them up for the risks they take.

1091
00:47:17,960 --> 00:47:20,960
And we'll call that money capital.

1092
00:47:20,960 --> 00:47:22,960
Okay, it's not money, it's not cash,

1093
00:47:22,960 --> 00:47:24,960
but it's assets that they can use

1094
00:47:24,960 --> 00:47:26,960
to get them out of trouble

1095
00:47:26,960 --> 00:47:28,960
if the risky assets do badly.

1096
00:47:28,960 --> 00:47:30,960
So if we're going to make,

1097
00:47:30,960 --> 00:47:32,960
we're going to have a requirement

1098
00:47:32,960 --> 00:47:34,960
on how much capital a bank holds.

1099
00:47:34,960 --> 00:47:38,960
We have also to define their risks.

1100
00:47:38,960 --> 00:47:42,960
And so Basel I had a very simple

1101
00:47:42,960 --> 00:47:46,960
formula to compute risk weighted assets.

1102
00:47:46,960 --> 00:47:47,960
Well, it's very simple

1103
00:47:47,960 --> 00:47:49,960
until you get into all the details.

1104
00:47:49,960 --> 00:47:52,960
And so you'll see the definition

1105
00:47:52,960 --> 00:47:54,960
of risk weighted assets.

1106
00:47:54,960 --> 00:47:59,960
It's in table 3.3 in Fibozzi.

1107
00:47:59,960 --> 00:48:01,960
But basically, here it is.

1108
00:48:01,960 --> 00:48:04,960
There's four categories of assets.

1109
00:48:04,960 --> 00:48:07,960
The 0% weight, the 20% weight,

1110
00:48:07,960 --> 00:48:11,960
the 50% weight and the 100% weight.

1111
00:48:11,960 --> 00:48:12,960
The higher the weight,

1112
00:48:12,960 --> 00:48:14,960
it means more risky.

1113
00:48:14,960 --> 00:48:16,960
Okay?

1114
00:48:16,960 --> 00:48:19,960
So where will I write these?

1115
00:48:19,960 --> 00:48:21,960
I'll do it up here.

1116
00:48:21,960 --> 00:48:23,960
Now, we're talking about all three,

1117
00:48:23,960 --> 00:48:26,960
Basel I, II and III.

1118
00:48:26,960 --> 00:48:32,960
0% are national,

1119
00:48:32,960 --> 00:48:37,960
well, I'll say OECD government bonds,

1120
00:48:37,960 --> 00:48:40,960
national government bonds.

1121
00:48:40,960 --> 00:48:41,960
The OECD is the

1122
00:48:41,960 --> 00:48:46,960
Organization for European Cooperation and Development.

1123
00:48:46,960 --> 00:48:50,960
And they represent advanced, stable European countries.

1124
00:48:50,960 --> 00:48:53,960
And U.S. government bonds

1125
00:48:53,960 --> 00:48:55,960
are included among them.

1126
00:48:55,960 --> 00:48:56,960
Okay.

1127
00:48:56,960 --> 00:48:57,960
What else?

1128
00:48:57,960 --> 00:49:01,960
It's basically this.

1129
00:49:01,960 --> 00:49:02,960
Those are the,

1130
00:49:02,960 --> 00:49:06,960
yeah, basically that's it.

1131
00:49:06,960 --> 00:49:07,960
Now, there might be something else in there.

1132
00:49:07,960 --> 00:49:08,960
And I'm sure there is.

1133
00:49:08,960 --> 00:49:10,960
But this is the simplest.

1134
00:49:10,960 --> 00:49:13,960
0% rate, because these have 0% risk.

1135
00:49:13,960 --> 00:49:14,960
There's no risk.

1136
00:49:14,960 --> 00:49:15,960
Okay?

1137
00:49:15,960 --> 00:49:17,960
So, banks can hold all they want of these,

1138
00:49:17,960 --> 00:49:20,960
and they don't have to hold any capital.

1139
00:49:20,960 --> 00:49:24,960
Then, next up is 20% weight.

1140
00:49:24,960 --> 00:49:27,960
And that's municipal bonds.

1141
00:49:27,960 --> 00:49:32,960
Now, or local bonds.

1142
00:49:32,960 --> 00:49:34,960
That's not issued by the national governments,

1143
00:49:34,960 --> 00:49:36,960
but issued by a city or a state.

1144
00:49:36,960 --> 00:49:39,960
We're having a municipal bond crisis now.

1145
00:49:39,960 --> 00:49:41,960
They're suddenly showing their risk.

1146
00:49:41,960 --> 00:49:43,960
And we're worried about defaults on them.

1147
00:49:43,960 --> 00:49:47,960
So, Basel I was right to give them some weight.

1148
00:49:47,960 --> 00:49:50,960
They gave them a 20% weight, because they thought

1149
00:49:50,960 --> 00:49:53,960
municipal bonds are pretty safe.

1150
00:49:53,960 --> 00:49:56,960
They're not as safe as national government bonds,

1151
00:49:56,960 --> 00:49:59,960
because there's examples of defaults.

1152
00:49:59,960 --> 00:50:01,960
But they also included,

1153
00:50:01,960 --> 00:50:04,960
there's a long list of what they include,

1154
00:50:04,960 --> 00:50:07,960
but notably, Fannie and Freddie.

1155
00:50:07,960 --> 00:50:08,960
Where,

1156
00:50:08,960 --> 00:50:11,960
these are the two mortgage lenders in the United States,

1157
00:50:11,960 --> 00:50:14,960
were included for 20% weight.

1158
00:50:14,960 --> 00:50:15,960
Because people thought, these guys

1159
00:50:15,960 --> 00:50:16,960
are really safe in any way.

1160
00:50:16,960 --> 00:50:18,960
The U.S. government backs them up.

1161
00:50:18,960 --> 00:50:21,960
Although the U.S. government said it wouldn't back them up.

1162
00:50:21,960 --> 00:50:23,460
But you know, we all know

1163
00:50:23,460 --> 00:50:24,960
they're going to back them up.

1164
00:50:24,960 --> 00:50:27,960
And indeed, they did back them up when they failed.

1165
00:50:27,960 --> 00:50:32,960
But Fannie and Freddie, prior to the crisis,

1166
00:50:32,960 --> 00:50:36,960
started increasingly investing in subprime mortgages.

1167
00:50:38,960 --> 00:50:43,960
And they were issuing subprime mortgage securities

1168
00:50:43,960 --> 00:50:47,960
that were really very risky, and eventually went kaput.

1169
00:50:47,960 --> 00:50:49,960
Basel I didn't know that.

1170
00:50:49,960 --> 00:50:51,960
Or Basel II and Basel III still,

1171
00:50:51,960 --> 00:50:53,960
they just give them a 20% weight.

1172
00:50:53,960 --> 00:50:55,960
It was a big mistake.

1173
00:50:55,960 --> 00:50:58,960
That's where part of the banking crisis comes.

1174
00:50:58,960 --> 00:51:01,960
Then there's 50% weight,

1175
00:51:01,960 --> 00:51:04,960
and that's for mortgages, home mortgages.

1176
00:51:04,960 --> 00:51:07,960
The Basel people thought,

1177
00:51:07,960 --> 00:51:10,960
there could be some big real estate crisis.

1178
00:51:10,960 --> 00:51:13,960
So, you know, it's something to worry about.

1179
00:51:13,960 --> 00:51:15,960
So, there's more weight than that.

1180
00:51:15,960 --> 00:51:19,960
And then we have 100% on everything else,

1181
00:51:19,960 --> 00:51:24,960
but notably loans, like commercial loans to businesses.

1182
00:51:24,960 --> 00:51:26,960
All right.

1183
00:51:29,960 --> 00:51:31,960
So, those are the weights.

1184
00:51:31,960 --> 00:51:33,460
And so, I just wanted

1185
00:51:33,460 --> 00:51:34,960
to go through a simple example.

1186
00:51:34,960 --> 00:51:36,960
Suppose you are a bank.

1187
00:51:37,960 --> 00:51:44,960
And you have $400 million in assets

1188
00:51:44,960 --> 00:51:46,960
on your balance sheet, okay?

1189
00:51:46,960 --> 00:51:48,960
These are things that you as a bank own.

1190
00:51:48,960 --> 00:51:52,960
And let's say you have 100 in government bonds,

1191
00:51:52,960 --> 00:51:54,960
federal government bonds,

1192
00:51:54,960 --> 00:51:57,960
100 in Fannie Mae,

1193
00:51:57,960 --> 00:51:59,960
100 million.

1194
00:51:59,960 --> 00:52:02,960
And you have 100 in mortgages

1195
00:52:02,960 --> 00:52:04,960
that you own directly.

1196
00:52:04,960 --> 00:52:06,960
And you have 100,

1197
00:52:06,960 --> 00:52:09,960
and 100 in commercial loans.

1198
00:52:12,960 --> 00:52:16,960
So, your total assets are 400 million.

1199
00:52:16,960 --> 00:52:19,960
But you got to know what your risk-weighted assets are.

1200
00:52:19,960 --> 00:52:21,960
So, what are your risk-weighted assets?

1201
00:52:21,960 --> 00:52:23,960
Well, I take the 100.

1202
00:52:23,960 --> 00:52:24,960
Fibozzi goes through an example too,

1203
00:52:24,960 --> 00:52:26,960
but this is very easy.

1204
00:52:26,960 --> 00:52:30,960
I multiply the 100 by zero, I get zero.

1205
00:52:30,960 --> 00:52:34,960
I multiply this 100 by 20%.

1206
00:52:34,960 --> 00:52:38,960
It gives me 20 million, right?

1207
00:52:38,960 --> 00:52:40,960
I multiply the mortgages by 50%.

1208
00:52:40,960 --> 00:52:42,960
It gives me 50 million.

1209
00:52:42,960 --> 00:52:45,960
And I got to throw all these in.

1210
00:52:45,960 --> 00:52:47,960
So, what does that add up to?

1211
00:52:47,960 --> 00:52:50,960
It's 170 million.

1212
00:52:50,960 --> 00:52:53,960
RWA, risk-weighted assets.

1213
00:52:53,960 --> 00:52:54,960
Right?

1214
00:52:54,960 --> 00:52:58,960
It's 20, zero plus 20 plus 50 plus 100.

1215
00:52:58,960 --> 00:53:00,960
All right?

1216
00:53:00,960 --> 00:53:02,960
So, those are my risk-weighted assets.

1217
00:53:02,960 --> 00:53:05,460
And then, the amount of

1218
00:53:05,460 --> 00:53:07,960
capital that I have to hold

1219
00:53:07,960 --> 00:53:11,960
is a percentage of the risk-weighted assets.

1220
00:53:11,960 --> 00:53:12,960
I don't want to go through,

1221
00:53:12,960 --> 00:53:14,960
I could go through Basel I, Basel II, Basel III.

1222
00:53:14,960 --> 00:53:16,960
They kept changing these percentages

1223
00:53:16,960 --> 00:53:18,960
and it's, as they went along.

1224
00:53:18,960 --> 00:53:21,960
So, I'm just going to talk about Basel III,

1225
00:53:21,960 --> 00:53:22,960
because that's going forward.

1226
00:53:22,960 --> 00:53:24,960
All right?

1227
00:53:24,960 --> 00:53:27,960
So, Basel, and Basel III is complicated too.

1228
00:53:27,960 --> 00:53:30,960
I'm going to just talk to you about

1229
00:53:30,960 --> 00:53:32,960
common equity requirements.

1230
00:53:32,960 --> 00:53:39,960
So, Basel III says,

1231
00:53:39,960 --> 00:53:42,960
and it's an interesting and creative construct.

1232
00:53:42,960 --> 00:53:59,960
Common equity must be 4.5% of RWA at all times.

1233
00:53:59,960 --> 00:54:01,960
But, I'm going to go through this.

1234
00:54:01,960 --> 00:54:03,960
But, I'll add to that.

1235
00:54:03,960 --> 00:54:10,960
They have plus 2.5%,

1236
00:54:10,960 --> 00:54:22,960
which they call capital conservation buffer.

1237
00:54:22,960 --> 00:54:27,960
And so, that adds up to 7%.

1238
00:54:27,960 --> 00:54:29,960
I'll explain.

1239
00:54:29,960 --> 00:54:30,960
You absolutely have to have 4.5%.

1240
00:54:30,960 --> 00:54:33,960
That's common equity.

1241
00:54:33,960 --> 00:54:38,960
But, if you don't also have another 2.5%,

1242
00:54:38,960 --> 00:54:40,960
you can't pay out any dividends.

1243
00:54:40,960 --> 00:54:42,960
That's not so good.

1244
00:54:42,960 --> 00:54:46,960
So, in reality, you better keep 7%.

1245
00:54:46,960 --> 00:54:49,960
So, effectively, Basel III,

1246
00:54:49,960 --> 00:54:51,960
this is Basel III.

1247
00:54:51,960 --> 00:54:54,960
Not in your textbook,

1248
00:54:54,960 --> 00:54:59,960
but it's coming all over the world.

1249
00:55:02,960 --> 00:55:04,960
Now, incidentally, the interesting thing about Basel III,

1250
00:55:04,960 --> 00:55:06,960
they're thinking creatively.

1251
00:55:06,960 --> 00:55:09,960
They added another buffer

1252
00:55:09,960 --> 00:55:12,960
called the counter-cyclical buffer.

1253
00:55:12,960 --> 00:55:18,960
Well, that's not added automatically.

1254
00:55:18,960 --> 00:55:23,960
And that's another 2.5%.

1255
00:55:23,960 --> 00:55:26,460
But, only if the regulators in

1256
00:55:26,460 --> 00:55:28,960
the country choose to impose it.

1257
00:55:28,960 --> 00:55:31,960
And here's the idea.

1258
00:55:31,960 --> 00:55:35,960
We have to stop bubbles before they burst, right?

1259
00:55:35,960 --> 00:55:37,960
So, suppose you think that a

1260
00:55:37,960 --> 00:55:39,960
bubble is building up in your country.

1261
00:55:39,960 --> 00:55:42,960
Then, the regulators are asked by Basel,

1262
00:55:42,960 --> 00:55:43,960
if they make that judgment,

1263
00:55:43,960 --> 00:55:47,960
to add another 2.5% to the capital requirement.

1264
00:55:47,960 --> 00:55:49,960
While it's booming.

1265
00:55:49,960 --> 00:55:50,960
You do this while it's,

1266
00:55:50,960 --> 00:55:52,960
you don't wait until the crisis to do this,

1267
00:55:52,960 --> 00:55:54,960
because then they'll all be in trouble.

1268
00:55:54,960 --> 00:55:56,960
And if you tighten up on banks then,

1269
00:55:56,960 --> 00:55:58,960
they'll stop making loans.

1270
00:55:58,960 --> 00:56:00,960
And they'll trash the whole economy.

1271
00:56:00,960 --> 00:56:02,960
You gotta tighten up when times are good.

1272
00:56:02,960 --> 00:56:07,960
So, that adds up to 9.5%.

1273
00:56:07,960 --> 00:56:11,960
Okay, so you'd have to hold capital equal to 9.5%

1274
00:56:11,960 --> 00:56:13,960
of your risk-weighted assets.

1275
00:56:13,960 --> 00:56:16,960
But presumably, the normal number is 7%.

1276
00:56:16,960 --> 00:56:18,960
Okay, so let me just

1277
00:56:18,960 --> 00:56:20,960
go through for this bank here,

1278
00:56:20,960 --> 00:56:24,960
which has $400 million in assets.

1279
00:56:24,960 --> 00:56:26,960
What is their requirement?

1280
00:56:26,960 --> 00:56:28,960
Well, we figured out,

1281
00:56:28,960 --> 00:56:32,960
that they have $170 million in risk-weighted assets.

1282
00:56:32,960 --> 00:56:37,960
Multiply that by 7%.

1283
00:56:37,960 --> 00:56:38,960
And that gives you,

1284
00:56:38,960 --> 00:56:43,960
I think it's $11.9 million that they have.

1285
00:56:43,960 --> 00:56:44,960
Right?

1286
00:56:44,960 --> 00:56:51,960
So, your common equity must be $11.9 million.

1287
00:56:55,960 --> 00:56:56,960
So, you go to your balance sheet.

1288
00:56:56,960 --> 00:56:58,960
We showed balance sheets in an earlier session.

1289
00:56:58,960 --> 00:57:01,960
It's common equity plus the buffer,

1290
00:57:01,960 --> 00:57:02,960
plus the first buffer.

1291
00:57:02,960 --> 00:57:03,960
I'm sorry.

1292
00:57:03,960 --> 00:57:05,960
The 11.9 is the...

1293
00:57:05,960 --> 00:57:07,960
Oh, did I do that with...

1294
00:57:07,960 --> 00:57:08,960
Yeah, I'm sorry.

1295
00:57:08,960 --> 00:57:14,960
That's 7% of $170 million.

1296
00:57:14,960 --> 00:57:16,960
Yeah, it does have...

1297
00:57:16,960 --> 00:57:18,960
You don't actually have to do...

1298
00:57:18,960 --> 00:57:21,960
Yeah, you don't actually have to hold this buffer.

1299
00:57:21,960 --> 00:57:23,960
But it limits you if you don't.

1300
00:57:23,960 --> 00:57:26,960
And so, practically, most banks will.

1301
00:57:26,960 --> 00:57:28,960
So, then you go look on your balance sheet.

1302
00:57:28,960 --> 00:57:30,960
You see, hey, we're lucky.

1303
00:57:30,960 --> 00:57:32,960
We've got $12.9 million.

1304
00:57:32,960 --> 00:57:33,960
Let's say.

1305
00:57:33,960 --> 00:57:34,960
I just made that up.

1306
00:57:34,960 --> 00:57:35,960
All right.

1307
00:57:35,960 --> 00:57:36,960
How do you get common equity?

1308
00:57:36,960 --> 00:57:39,960
You take the total assets in your balance sheet.

1309
00:57:39,960 --> 00:57:42,960
You subtract off all of the liabilities,

1310
00:57:42,960 --> 00:57:44,960
all the money you owed.

1311
00:57:44,960 --> 00:57:45,960
And that gives you shareholders' equity.

1312
00:57:45,960 --> 00:57:47,960
But that has two components,

1313
00:57:47,960 --> 00:57:49,960
common equity and preferred equity.

1314
00:57:49,960 --> 00:57:51,960
So, you've got to subtract off the preferred equity.

1315
00:57:51,960 --> 00:57:53,960
But it's a sense of

1316
00:57:53,960 --> 00:57:55,960
how much extra resources you have

1317
00:57:55,960 --> 00:57:57,960
after you've paid off all your debts.

1318
00:57:57,960 --> 00:58:01,960
You still have $12.9 million.

1319
00:58:01,960 --> 00:58:03,960
So, that gives...

1320
00:58:03,960 --> 00:58:06,960
You say, hey, we're good.

1321
00:58:06,960 --> 00:58:08,960
We've got an extra million dollars.

1322
00:58:08,960 --> 00:58:09,960
Okay.

1323
00:58:09,960 --> 00:58:10,960
So, then you bring that up

1324
00:58:10,960 --> 00:58:11,960
at the board meeting and say,

1325
00:58:11,960 --> 00:58:13,960
we're satisfying Basel III.

1326
00:58:13,960 --> 00:58:14,960
Isn't that great?

1327
00:58:14,960 --> 00:58:16,960
But someone at the board might say,

1328
00:58:16,960 --> 00:58:17,960
but wait a minute.

1329
00:58:17,960 --> 00:58:20,960
That means we have a million dollars too much.

1330
00:58:20,960 --> 00:58:21,960
It's just sitting there.

1331
00:58:21,960 --> 00:58:23,960
We're not even using it.

1332
00:58:23,960 --> 00:58:24,960
Let's lend it out.

1333
00:58:24,960 --> 00:58:25,960
All right.

1334
00:58:25,960 --> 00:58:26,960
Let's...

1335
00:58:26,960 --> 00:58:27,960
Well, I'm sorry.

1336
00:58:27,960 --> 00:58:29,960
Let's use it up, not lend it out.

1337
00:58:29,960 --> 00:58:30,960
How do you use it up?

1338
00:58:30,960 --> 00:58:31,960
You use it up.

1339
00:58:31,960 --> 00:58:34,960
Now, you've got more than the amount of capital required.

1340
00:58:34,960 --> 00:58:36,960
You've got another million dollars.

1341
00:58:36,960 --> 00:58:38,960
You could lend out a million dollars.

1342
00:58:38,960 --> 00:58:39,960
But think about it.

1343
00:58:39,960 --> 00:58:43,960
You can borrow more and lend more.

1344
00:58:43,960 --> 00:58:45,960
You can make loans.

1345
00:58:45,960 --> 00:58:48,960
Or you can borrow more and take other assets.

1346
00:58:48,960 --> 00:58:51,960
And you can go beyond a million dollars.

1347
00:58:51,960 --> 00:58:53,960
So, here's what you can...

1348
00:58:53,960 --> 00:58:54,960
Let's consider this.

1349
00:58:54,960 --> 00:58:55,960
So, do you understand the

1350
00:58:55,960 --> 00:58:56,960
situation that we've done our accounting?

1351
00:58:56,960 --> 00:59:00,960
We have 170 million in risk-weighted assets.

1352
00:59:00,960 --> 00:59:03,960
We have a requirement, therefore,

1353
00:59:03,960 --> 00:59:06,960
of 11.9 million in common equity.

1354
00:59:06,960 --> 00:59:08,960
We have an extra million dollars.

1355
00:59:08,960 --> 00:59:11,960
Let's consider buying different kinds of assets.

1356
00:59:11,960 --> 00:59:14,960
How about buying Fannie Mae bonds?

1357
00:59:14,960 --> 00:59:15,960
All right.

1358
00:59:15,960 --> 00:59:19,960
Now, remember, you only...

1359
00:59:19,960 --> 00:59:21,960
How much more can you buy?

1360
00:59:21,960 --> 00:59:23,960
Well, you've got a million dollars.

1361
00:59:23,960 --> 00:59:24,960
If you're going to buy

1362
00:59:24,960 --> 00:59:25,960
more, you're going to add both.

1363
00:59:25,960 --> 00:59:26,960
You're going to borrow money,

1364
